rectly connected to the immune-related adverse event profile of this modality. [Data: Entities (681, 728); Relationships (958)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Immune-related adverse events (irAEs), including autoimmune reactions, are a central safety and compliance consideration for immunotherapy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community explicitly connects immunotherapy to immune-related adverse events (irAEs), describing them as side effects of immunotherapy/immune checkpoint inhibitors caused by immune activation affecting normal tissues. This makes toxicity management an intrinsic part of immunotherapy deployment. [Data: Entities (728); Relationships (994, 958)].\\n\\nAutoimmune reaction is listed as a possible side effect of immunotherapy, underscoring that the same immune activation sought for anti-tumor benefit can also drive harmful self-directed immune effects. This elevates the importance of patient selection and monitoring as an operational requirement. [Data: Entities (5985); Relationships (13660)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Hepatitis B reactivation risk creates a clear pre-treatment testing and monitoring pathway for immunotherapy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Hepatitis B virus is described as capable of persisting in the body, and immunotherapy is linked to the risk of “reawakening” (reactivating) HBV, potentially causing illness and interfering with treatment (including via liver damage that can complicate ongoing cancer care). [Data: Entities (2442, 4233); Relationships (8799, 8800)].\\n\\nHEPATITIS B TESTING is described as recommended and often necessary before starting immunotherapy for people with prior HBV infection or risk, specifically because immunotherapy can trigger reactivation. This creates a concrete compliance-oriented step in the treatment workflow: screen before initiating immunotherapy when risk factors exist. [Data: Entities (4220); Relationships (8798)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Baseline immune weakness (immunodeficiency, HIV/AIDS, immune suppression) is repeatedly linked to cancer/viral-risk pathways relevant to oncology decisions\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Immunodeficiency disorder is listed as a risk factor for primary CNS lymphoma, demonstrating a direct connection between impaired immune function and malignancy risk in this community. [Data: Entities (4104); Relationships (8521)].\\n\\nHIV/AIDS is provided as an example cause of a weakened immune system, reinforcing that specific clinical conditions are used to illustrate how immune competence can be diminished. [Data: Entities (4628, 119); Relationships (9963)].\\n\\nImmune suppression is connected to autoimmune diseases and organ transplants as causes of weakened immune systems, and it is also tied to impaired HPV clearance (harder for the body to clear HPV infection). Collectively, these relationships show that immune status is treated as both a risk modifier (for infection persistence) and a clinical background factor likely relevant to treatment safety and outcomes. [Data: Entities (22, 24, 23, 6017); Relationships (51, 50, 13750)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Core immune components (lymphatic system, T cells, NK cells) and immune cell markers underpin how immune activity is assessed and understood\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The lymphatic system is described as part of the immune system and helping fight infections and disease, reflecting the structural and functional anatomy supporting immune responses. [Data: Entities (1293, 119); Relationships (2129)].\\n\\nT lymphocytes (T cells) and natural killer (NK) cells are directly linked to immune system function, emphasizing that specific lymphocyte subsets are recognized for anti-infection and anti-cancer roles (T cells for control/coordination and NK cells for killing tumor or virus-infected cells). [Data: Entities (1265, 1266, 119); Relationships (2071, 2072)].\\n\\nCell markers are linked to the immune system in the context of immunophenotyping, indicating that measurement and classification of immune cells (e.g., via marker detection) is a technical capability used to characterize immune status and responses. [Data: Entities (1825, 119); Relationships (3040)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Treatment selection signals: biomarker-defined cancers and specific immunotherapy agents illustrate targeted use cases\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Immunotherapy with checkpoint inhibitors is described as preferred for dMMR/MSI-H cancers, illustrating that biomarker-defined subgroups can drive immunotherapy preference and sequencing decisions. [Data: Entities (4500); Relationships (9488)].\\n\\nCemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo) is named as an immunotherapy option that may be used first when surgery is risky or may not fully remove cancer, with the intent to shrink tumors and reduce surgical risk. This highlights immunotherapy’s role not only in metastatic settings but also potentially as a pre-surgical strategy in selected scenarios. [Data: Entities (850); Relationships (1257)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community also includes local chemotherapy delivery (intravesical treatment), showing multimodal cancer therapy context around immunotherapy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Chemotherapy is described as sometimes being delivered directly into the bladder as intravesical treatment, indicating a localized therapy approach that co-exists in the broader oncology landscape captured by the network. This provides context that immunotherapy-related concepts (e.g., checkpoint inhibitors) sit alongside established chemotherapy delivery strategies in cancer treatment planning. [Data: Entities (5760); Relationships (13003)].\\n\\nThis multimodal framing is further supported by the network’s inclusion of progression as a cancer course concept discussed in relation to adrenal cancer, indicating that treatment strategies may be reassessed in response to progression and can include consideration of immunother